| Other and nested emphasis tags marked
|   is retained and any modifications are appropriately noted below.
| States: 1 = outside any emphasis tags
|   2 = inside b tags
|   3 = inside it tags
|   4 = inside "other" tags
|   5 = inside comment tag (within the tag itself) in outer state 1
|   6 = inside comment tag (within the tag itself) in outer state 2
|   7 = inside comment tag (within the tag itself) in outer state 3
|   8 = inside comment tag (within the tag itself) in outer state 4
|   9 = inside "strong" tags
|  10 = inside comment tag (within the tag itself) in outer state 9
|  11 = inside "em" tags
|  12 = inside comment tag (within the tag itself) in outer state 11
| 1;1 1993-5-31 J E Sullivan
| 2;1 1995-12-28 JES: to simplify treatment of HTML, map <b> and <other>
|     to <it>, and simply delete rather than mark any nestings
| 2;2 1998-9-23 JES: treat <strong> similarly to <other>
|     2007-8-17 MPG: Remove mapping of <other> to <it>
| 3;1 2012-07-10 JES: Change mapping of <strong> from <it> to <b>;
|     remove mapping of <b> to <it> (remains <b>);
|     add mapping of <em> to <it> (all these consistent with MS IE normal
|     treatment, and necessary now that this is being used for imports
|     other than ICADD and HTML)
| 3;2 2012-10-19 JES: correct treatment of <em> -- was generating <it>
|     but not corresponding </it>
| SGML comments:
<!--~<!--~1~5~ | beginning of comment to be flushed through
-->~-->~5~1~ | end of comment
<!--~<!--~2~6~ | beginning of comment to be flushed through
-->~-->~6~2~ | end of comment
<!--~<!--~3~7~ | beginning of comment to be flushed through
-->~-->~7~3~ | end of comment
<!--~<!--~4~8~ | beginning of comment to be flushed through
-->~-->~8~4~ | end of comment
<!--~<!--~9~10~ | beginning of comment to be flushed through
-->~-->~10~9~ | end of comment
<!--~<!--~11~12~ | beginning of comment to be flushed through
-->~-->~12~1~ | end of comment
| Rules for state 1:
<b>~<b>~1~2~
</b>~~1~
<it>~<it>~1~3~
</it>~~1~
<other>~<other>~1~4~
</other>~~1~
<strong>~<b>~1~9~
</strong>~~1~
<em>~<it>~1~11~
</em>~~1~
| Rules for state 2:
<b>~~2~
</b>~</b>~2~1~
